  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Geography
    
    A geothermally heated crater-lagoon at the Berg's far edge, where the ice curves inward and the Veil Vents — volcanic heat sources of uncertain origin — have pumped warmth into standing water for longer than any living record covers. The crater walls rise in shelves of blue-black ice. The water is jade-green, heavy with dissolved minerals that stain anything immersed and leave a metallic taste. Its surface distorts reflections in ways that have generated superstitious commentary I will not reproduce here. Steam rises in dense columns, though this density has measurably decreased in recent cycles.
    
    The perimeter ice, by contrast, is remarkably clear — visible to ten fathoms — and crystal spires near the crater's edge resonate at specific aurora frequencies. This optical clarity at the margins and opacity at the center is consistent with differential mineral concentration, though no formal survey has been conducted. No formal survey has been *permitted*.
    
    ## Pre-Settlement History
    
    The crater predates its current inhabitants by a considerable margin. The Nightshelf weapon ☽'micseleia', a shadow-steel dagger whose fight-memories have been catalogued in the Nightshelf Provenance Register Vol. 65, carries impressions from the Still Pools that include a blood-ward in which the blade's steel cracked from within — suggesting organized defensive works at or near the site well before any dissidents arrived. The Register classifies the dagger's fracture as irreparable and widening, which is relevant to provenance scholars if not to this entry.
    
    Runoff channels from the Berg's deeper tunnel network drain into the crater. Nightshelf workers historically used these channels to wash residue from the Bastion Apex, a crystal formation in the tunnels above, meaning the Still Pools' water supply was integrated into Nightshelf infrastructure long before the settlement existed. Mining charts of uncertain provenance indicate a branch passage extending beneath the crater to extreme depth — farther, the notation suggests, than standard Nightshelf survey protocols permit recording.
    
    The Aureate merchant guild, now administrators of the Frost Zenith, traces its origins to the Still Pools during the Clever Ice Age. Bursar Ingnao shi'Beacon, the guild's founding ledger-keeper, operated from the crater before his passing. The settlement's relationship to this mercantile history is unclear; the current residents do not appear to claim it.
    
    ## The Dissident Settlement
    
    The colony's present population consists of Aurora Stack dissidents — perhaps three hundred, perhaps fewer — who settled the crater generations ago in rejection of the Flipper Accord, crystal-worship, and dependency on deep fire-cores. No census has counted them. No official map marks their location. The residents call the settlement **"the Forgetting,"** not because they have forgotten the world but because they believed the world had forgotten them.
    
    Construction is layered: wooden platforms replaced as they rot, ice-carved dwellings oriented along the thermal gradient, fishing platforms at the water's edge. The ice-gardens — plots of soil hauled from deeper in the Berg — sustain root-vegetables, copper-tasting fibrous plants, and bioluminescent fungi in the thermal haze. Initials are carved into the platforms with dates and single words: *stayed*, *left*, *drowned*. Offerings of fish bones and ice-shards appear at the crater's highest point, left by persons unknown.
    
    The founding generation is dying. Their children and grandchildren have never seen Aurora Stack or felt sky-light on their feathers.
    
    Local lullabies reference the warm pools and ice-gardens but trail into verses about those who "walked far away and came back changed" — an allusion to the Walking, which the nestkeepers refuse to explain. Other songs encode the settlement's crisis: warmth and safety promised in early verses, steam thinning to nothing in the final lines. The adults go quiet when fledglings reach those endings.
    
    ## Ceremonial and Political Function
    
    The Still Pools also serve as the traditional site for **Circle Gatherings** — formal multi-faction assemblies held under aurora light, where delegations arrange rings on the ice and the Luminaries preside. Nightshelf delegations position themselves at the far end; surface factions such as the E'frostlings claim positions nearer center. The **Clear-sky Mandate**, a war-measure from the Orca Incursion requiring Council votes occur only under clear aurora skies, was enacted here.
    
    I note the contradiction without resolving it: the settlement maintains a strict policy of no outside contact and is protected by the Nightshelf's Mandatum∴absconditu — which explicitly forbids mapping, census-taking, or speaking the settlement's name where steam does not cover the voice — yet the crater hosts formal assemblies attended by every major faction on the Berg. Either the Gatherings occur at a location distinct from the settlement proper, or "no contact" has always meant something more selective than the word implies. The Nightshelf records, characteristically, clarify nothing.
    
    ## Compromise and Threat
    
    Despite the isolation policy, anonymous job postings have surfaced on Aurora Stack notice boards seeking skilled geothermal workers — pressure calibration, mineral-flow assessment — offering payment in warm-water fish. Recruitment operates through a copper-stained courier at the Third Spire docks, with instructions to exclude the Luminaries' Watchers. The Veil Vents are failing. The steam that once blinded scouting parties has thinned enough to see the far crater wall.
    
    Corruption from the Glow-Fissure beneath Nightfall Shelf has been detected bleeding into the crater, prompting joint sanctification agreements between the Aureate guild and the ☽'shadow Covens' Order. The taint has not stayed contained; frost-wailing and cold-burn discoloration have spread outward for cycles, reaching ice-shelves as distant as the Forum Floe.
    
    The waters beyond the settlement's shallows have served as hunting cover for orca raiders exploiting the stagnant pressure and depth. The orca raider Krathund Gnurl attempted the Grulkzul Omega Strike — a blood-working fueled by stolen Nightshelf fire-knowledge — in the nearby waters, rupturing the ice shelf along a previously nonexistent fault line and scattering artifacts across the deep. His pod-mate Deep Maw Krixizak remains in the waters, the sole survivor.
    
    The settlement that believed itself forgotten is now threatened from below, from without, and from the slow failure of the very phenomenon that made hiding possible.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
