  annotation "note_1771574700815_1", anchor: "perhaps three hundred, perhaps fewer", text: "Three hundred. From a settlement no census has counted, that appears on no map, protected by a doctrine forbidding anyone from counting them. This is not an estimate. This is a guess wearing an estimate's coat.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771574700815_2", anchor: "anonymous job postings have surfaced on Aurora Stack notice boards seeking skilled geothermal workers", text: "Strict policy of no outside contact. Recruitment advertisements at Third Spire docks. Payment terms specified. Exclusion lists curated. I have catalogued eight hundred years of penguin self-deception in these archives and I confess this one charms me. They are not breaking isolation — they are *outsourcing within it.*", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771574700815_3", anchor: "trail into verses about those who \"walked far away and came back changed\"", text: "Third instance now — after Kelm's counting games and the Forgotten Shore boundary rhymes — of children's oral tradition encoding data that the institutional record refuses to hold. Here the lullabies document Walking incidents at a settlement no official body acknowledges, meaning these verses constitute the *only* surviving record of the Walking's incidence at the Berg's far edge. The nestkeepers who refuse to explain the final verses to their fledglings understand perfectly well what they mean. They are not protecting innocence. They are curating the archive. See my notes on the Forgotten Shore for the broader pattern; it is no longer a pattern, it is an infrastructure.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771574700815_4", anchor: "The Aureate merchant guild, now administrators of the Frost Zenith, traces its origins to the Still Pools during the Clever Ice Age", text: "The wealthiest merchant guild on the Berg — crystal-ambitious, ledger-obsessive, holders of the Frost Zenith — was born in a geothermally heated hideaway whose current residents reject commerce, refuse the Accord, and call themselves 'the Forgetting.' Bursar Ingnao would have appreciated the irony. Or possibly not. The ice-impressions of him suggest a man with very little sense of humor about money.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771574700815_5", anchor: "No formal survey has been *permitted*", text: "The seal-order is in the administrative ice from the Clever Ice Age, fourth sub-layer, alongside three others covering sites now lost. Unsigned, which in Nightshelf practice means signed by someone whose name the record is not permitted to hold. I found it between a requisition for mineral-testing equipment and the requisition's cancellation. Someone ordered the tools, then ordered the tools unmade. The interval between the two filings is less than a single tidal cycle.", type: :correction, display: :full
